```

Helper at lines 326-329:

```java
private static String generateMembershipNumber(String customerCode, LocalDate registrationDate) {
    String yy = String.format("%02d", registrationDate.getYear() % 100);
    return customerCode + "-M" + yy;
}
```

The **year segment** `YY` is the last two digits of the resolved `registrationDate`'s year (e.g. `SYD-SMI-0007-M26`). The `customerCode` prefix is built by `generateCustomerCode` (lines 288-293) as `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>`, set at line 145 just before the membership number.

## 4. Where the daily create-limit is enforced

`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`, in `addOwner` lines 133-135:

```java
if (countOwnersRegisteredOn(LocalDate.now()) >= 100) {
    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS);
}
```

The counting helper `countOwnersRegisteredOn` at lines 345-349:

```java
private int countOwnersRegisteredOn(LocalDate date) {
    return (int) this.clinicService.findAllOwners().stream()
        .filter(existing -> date.equals(existing.getRegistrationDate()))
        .count();
}
```

Limit is 100 owners per day, returning HTTP 429 (TOO_MANY_REQUESTS). Note it counts against `LocalDate.now()` (server today), not against the resolved `registrationDate` of the incoming owner.

## 5. Existing tests related to these features

Only one acceptance test class currently exercises this area:

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` — test `coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay` (lines 14-19):

```java
@Test
void coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay() throws Exception {
    int id = createOwnerOk(ownerNode());
    DayOfWeek dow = LocalDate.parse(fetchOwner(id).get("registrationDate").asText()).getDayOfWeek();
    assertTrue(dow != DayOfWeek.SATURDAY && dow != DayOfWeek.SUNDAY, dow.toString());
}
```

The class comment (lines 9-11) states the spec: "the default registration date must fall on a business day (a weekend rolls forward to Monday) ... the default registrationDate is never a Saturday or Sunday."

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — supporting harness:
  - `createMany(int n)` (lines 181-187) — creates N owners all dated today, described as driving "the per-day create-limit / bulk-signup rules."
  - `toBusinessDay(LocalDate)` (lines 267-274) — rolls a date forward over weekends and pinned `HOLIDAYS` to the next business day.
  - `HOLIDAYS` set (lines 240-242) — fixed 2026 public holidays the business-day roll skips.
  - `sha256hex` (lines 282-289) and the "computed expectations" section replicate the household-id/customer-code derivations.

No tests directly assert the membership-number format/year-segment or the 429 create-limit yet; the harness helpers (`createMany`, `toBusinessDay`) exist to support such tests.

### Notable behavioral gap
The test `coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay` and the base helper `toBusinessDay` expect the **default** registration date to never fall on a weekend (roll forward to next business day). However, the controller's `resolveRegistrationDate` (lines 318-320) returns a bare `LocalDate.now()` with no weekend/holiday rolling. If the server date is a Saturday or Sunday, the stored default `registrationDate` would be a weekend day, contradicting the documented invariant — so this logic appears unimplemented relative to the spec the test encodes.

